  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Climate Adaptation to Address the Adverse Environmental Drivers of Migration in Agriculture- and Resource-Dependent Communities in Southern Mexico</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Environmental change and disasters have long impacted communities in Mexico, affecting approximately 68% of the population at some point in their lives. Such changes can negatively impact livelihoods, well-being, and human security. Climate variability affects growing seasons, soil quality, and water availability, diminishing crop quality and productivity. Poor agricultural communities face recurrent flooding and lack resources for risk reduction, exacerbating their vulnerability. Slash-and-burn practices and extreme weather conditions contribute to wildfires, while sea-level rise threatens coastal areas, exacerbating flooding and soil salination. Promoting traditional agricultural practices and building adaptive capacities are crucial strategies. Despite promising pilot initiatives, government response remains limited due to funding and institutional challenges. To address the challenges highlighted above, IOM, in collaboration with expert climate change adaptation and agricultural specialists in the Rainforest Alliance and a wide array of local and federal governmental, academic, private sector, UN, and civil society partners, will work together to achieve the following ultimate result: Promote climate resilient agricultural practices among developing communities in Southern Mexico as a way of increasing adaptation to climate change and addressing the adverse environmental drivers of migration, while promoting safe livelihoods and migration options for environmental migrants.</narrative>
  </description>
